36	    ///
37	    /// Enforcing [naming conventions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naming_convention_(programming)) helps to keep the codebase consistent,
38	    /// and reduces overhead when thinking about the name [case] of a variable.
39	    ///
40	    /// ## Naming conventions
41	    ///
42	    /// All names can be prefixed and suffixed by underscores `_` and dollar signs `$`.
43	    ///
44	    /// ### Variable names
45	    ///
46	    /// All variables, including function parameters and catch parameters, are in [`camelCase`].
47	    ///
48	    /// Additionally, top-level variables declared as `const` or `var` may be in [`CONSTANT_CASE`] or [`PascalCase`].
49	    /// Top-level variables are declared at module or script level.
50	    /// Variables declared in a TypeScript `module` or `namespace` are also considered top-level.
51	    ///
52	    /// ```js
53	    /// function f(param, _unusedParam) {
54	    ///     let localValue = 0;
55	    ///     try {
56	    ///         /* ... */
57	    ///     } catch (customError) {
58	    ///         /* ... */
59	    ///     }
60	    /// }
61	    ///
62	    /// export const A_CONSTANT = 5;
63	    ///
64	    /// export const Person = class {}
65	    ///
66	    /// let aVariable = 0;
67	    ///
68	    /// export namespace ns {
69	    ///     export const ANOTHER_CONSTANT = "";
70	    /// }
71	    /// ```
72	    ///
73	    /// Examples of incorrect names:
74	    ///
75	    /// ```js,expect_diagnostic
76	    /// let a_value = 0;
77	    /// ```
78	    ///
79	    /// ```js,expect_diagnostic
80	    /// const fooYPosition = 0;
81	    /// ```
82	    ///
83	    /// ```js,expect_diagnostic
84	    /// function f(FirstParam) {}
85	    /// ```
86	    ///
87	    /// ### Function names
88	    ///
89	    /// A `function` name is in [`camelCase`] or [`PascalCase`].
90	    ///
91	    /// ```jsx
92	    /// function trimString(s) { /*...*/ }
93	    ///
94	    /// function Component() {
95	    ///     return <div></div>;
96	    /// }
97	    /// ```
98	    ///
99	    /// ### TypeScript `enum` names
100	    ///
101	    /// A _TypeScript_ `enum` name is in [`PascalCase`].
102	    ///
103	    /// `enum` members are by default in [`PascalCase`].
104	    /// However, you can configure the [case] of `enum` members.
