# Diagnostic Messages

## requireAscii (Ascii) Suggestion

**Main message:**
> `"This "<Emphasis>{format_args!("{convention_selector}")}</Emphasis>" name should be in ASCII because "<Emphasis>"requireAscii"</Emphasis>" is set to `true`."`

**Note:**
> `"If you want to use non-ASCII names, then set the "<Emphasis>"requireAscii"</Emphasis>" option to `false`.\nSee the rule "<Hyperlink href="https://biomejs.dev/linter/rules/use-naming-convention#options">"options"</Hyperlink>" for more details."`

## strictCase Consecutive Uppercase

**Main message:**
> `"Two consecutive uppercase characters are not allowed in "{format_args!("{actual_case}")}" because "<Emphasis>"strictCase"</Emphasis>" is set to `true`."`

**Note:**
> `"If you want to use consecutive uppercase characters in "{format_args!("{actual_case}")}", then set the "<Emphasis>"strictCase"</Emphasis>" option to `false`.\nSee the rule "<Hyperlink href="https://biomejs.dev/linter/rules/use-naming-convention#options">"options"</Hyperlink>" for more details."`

---

# Handling in `run()`

## requireAscii check
The code retrieves options and, per the comments, checks the ASCII requirement. When enabled and the name is non-ASCII, it returns a `State` with `Suggestion::Ascii`:

```rust
if options.require_ascii() && !name.is_ascii() {
    return Some(State {
        convention_selector: Selector::default(),
        name_range: Range {
            start: 0u16,
            end: name.len() as u16,
        },
        suggestion: Suggestion::Ascii,
    });
}
```

## Underscore prefix / unused variable handling
Rather than "consecutive underscores," the run function handles a **leading underscore on unused variables**, ignoring them to avoid conflict with the `noUnusedVariables` rule:

```rust
if name.starts_with('_')
    && let Ok(binding) = &node.try_into()
    && is_unused(ctx.model(), binding)
{
    return None;
}
```

The inline comment explains this "Always ignore unused variables prefixed with `_`."

## Trailing/leading underscore & dollar trimming
Separately, before the default-convention check, the name is trimmed of underscores and dollar signs (only if not already trimmed via a capture group), via the helper `trim_underscore_dollar`:

```rust
if is_not_trimmed {
    let (prefix_len, trimmed_name) = trim_underscore_dollar(name);
    name_range_start += prefix_len;
    name = trimmed_name;
}
```

That helper counts leading bytes matching `_` or `$`, then counts trailing ones and slices them off, returning the prefix length and trimmed name.

---

**Note:** The strictCase consecutive-uppercase message appears in the `diagnostic()` function (not `run()`), triggered when `strict_case()` is enabled and the expected cases include Camel or Pascal, and the trimmed name identifies as Camel/Pascal loosely but as `Case::Unknown` under strict identification.

